{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>That it may be well with you, and that your days may be long.</b> This expression is also said regarding honoring father and mother in the Decalogue's second tablets, and the expression 'that it may be well with you' implies that good will automatically flow to you from this. Now, regarding honoring father and mother the matter is straightforward: if you honor your father and mother, then your children will learn from you and do likewise for you when they grow up; and so when your children see that you show honor to parents even in the case of birds, they will learn a fortiori — if you conduct yourself with honor toward those who give birth even among animals, how much more so will they conduct themselves with honor toward those who gave birth to them. And the reward for both is length of days, because these two commandments sustain the belief in the creation of the world anew, for just as every cause has a cause, and the matter chains back until the first Cause, blessed be He, so too everything born has one who gave it birth, back until the first Begetter, blessed be He — and to Him alone is it fitting to ascribe honor. And from His honor, blessed be He, He apportioned to all who beget, who chain back from Him, blessed be He, from cause to cause. Therefore Scripture everywhere mentions the observance of the Sabbath alongside the commandment of honoring father and mother, for the Sabbath, which comes as a remembrance of the world's creation anew, brings about the fulfillment of the commandment to honor father and mother, to refute the opinion of those who believe in primordiality, who say the world has no beginning at all and everything is necessary existence. And according to their benighted reasoning there is no superiority of the begetter over the begotten. But we believe that the first Begetter, blessed be He, apportioned of His honor to all who beget, who are influenced from Him, blessed be He — and this is the reason for honoring father and mother and for sending away the nest. And for this reason the commandment was juxtaposed to the commandment of 'When you build a new house,' and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tanhuma, Re'eh 1): If you have fulfilled the commandment of sending away the nest, you will merit building a new house. What does one have to do with the other? Rather, since the commandment of sending away the nest brings you to faith in the world's creation anew — that the Holy One, blessed be He, built it and renewed it — therefore measure for measure you too will merit building a new house. And this is the foundation of faith, as it is written (Habakkuk 2:4): ״וְצַדִּיק בֶּאֱמוּנָתוֹ יִחְיֶה״ ('the righteous shall live by his faith'), for through faith one cleaves to the Source of life; therefore his reward is length of days. And therefore they expounded (Bereshit Rabbati on Genesis) regarding sending away the nest the verse ״אֹרַח חַיִּים פֶּן תְּפַלֵּס״ (Proverbs 5:6)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The citation abbreviated בד״ר is resolved here as Bereshit Rabbati; it may alternatively refer to a minor midrashic collection. The verse cited from Proverbs 5:6 in standard editions reads לְמַעַן תִּשְׁמֹר אֹרַח חַיִּים, and the exact form the Kli Yakar quotes appears to reflect a variant textual tradition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:18.869627485Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}